        "source": "import triton\nimport triton.language as tl\n\n@triton.jit\ndef softmax_fused(X, Y, N: tl.constexpr, TOTAL: tl.constexpr, BLOCK: tl.constexpr):\n    row = tl.program_id(0)\n    block_id = tl.program_id(1)\n    # Use power-of-two BLOCK (64) for arange compatibility\n    offs_in_block = tl.arange(0, BLOCK)\n    col = block_id * BLOCK + offs_in_block\n\n    mask = col < N\n    offs = row * N + col\n\n    # Load input with masking for boundary\n    x = tl.load(X + offs, mask, other=-float('inf'))\n\n    # Compute max per row block (reduction over BLOCK dimension)\n    mx = tl.max(x, axis=0)\n\n    # Compute exp and sum for normalization\n    exps = tl.exp(x - mx)\n    denom = tl.sum(exps, axis=0)\n\n    # Normalize\n    y = exps / denom\n\n    # Store output with masking\n    tl.store(Y + offs, y, mask)\n",
        "rationale": "Corrected arange usage to use a power-of-two BLOCK=64 instead of 49 to fix compilation error. This preserves the previous fusion and 2D grid decomposition design to reduce kernel launches and memory traffic (K05), improves parallel reduction efficiency by using 8 warps (K06), and maintains masking to handle irregular shape boundaries without padding (K07). The power-of-two block size ensures arange compiles, enabling proper vectorization and memory coalescing (K01). This approach keeps the fusion benefits and addresses the compilation failure from the original attempt."
